america's longest war is over the  pentagon says the last u.s troops have  flown out of afghanistan after a 20-year  mission the departure comes after two  weeks of chaotic and desperate scenes in  and around kabul airport as thousands of  foreign nationals and afghan citizens  tried to flee the taliban have now taken  control of the airport and proclaimed  afghanistan's full independence  there's no doubt who's in control here  taliban fighters parade their win on the  tarmac of kabul's international airport  some even taking selfies from the  cockpit of an abandoned aircraft  first of all i would like to say that  afghanistan is finally in every sense  free and independent  and our forces are controlling the  military and civilian parts of the  airport  normal activity of the airport will be  started very soon  close by military aircraft  some apparently disabled in the rush by  american troops to get out of  afghanistan  as fast as possible  just hours earlier the commander of the  82nd airborne division became the last  u.s service member to leave  that prompted celebratory gunfire at the  airport to mark the ending of this  bitter 20-year war  alhamdulillah thank god that we took our  country's independence  all muslims and all afghanistan people  are happy and share the happiness with  us  taliban soldiers may be enjoying their  victory  but the chaos of the last desperate days  here is left plain for all to see  long queues continue to build up outside  kabul's banks  and there isn't a woman in sight  the taliban is expected to form a  government within the next few days  a chastened international community says  it's hopeful it won't be as extreme as  feared  but on the ground it's clear  who  holds the power  let's get an update on the situation in  kabul we can speak to journalist franz  marty now  france american troops have now fully  left the country after 20 years  what are things like in kabul today with  the taliban fully in control  i was briefly in the city center today  and there there wasn't much of a  difference to the past days so shops  were open people were going about their  business there were maybe a little bit  fewer people on the street uh certainly  fewer women but there were still women  around  um there is still like the taliban  haven't imposed like new rules or  something like that uh  most of it  continues as before  the atmosphere wasn't jubilant so  if one wouldn't have known that this  historic  moment happened the night before one  wouldn't have seen it  some shopkeepers told me that business  has broken down  and that they're worried about the  future  one street vendor also told me that he's  worried what what will come now  so it is still that like most afghans  seem to be wary about what the future  brings yeah a lot of uncertainty friends  we saw the pictures of the taliban now  fully in control of the airport as well  so what about foreign nationals and  afghans who want to leave the country  can they still do so  the taliban have several times assured  that foreign nationals and afghans with  the right travel papers are allowed to  depart the country  so far before it was because of the  evacuation operation the the airport was  like  as only operated by these evocation  flights and very restricted to get  inside now the civilian airport might  open sooner than like uh people might  have expected uh the taliban have  certainly interest and there are reports  that they get like support from qatar or  turkey uh to um again reopen the airport  how difficult it then will be for  foreigners or afghan nationals to leave  remains to be seen foreign nationals  probably no big issue  because the taliban don't have an  interest to hold them there afghans is  another story but one should also keep  in mind that the taliban probably also  don't want to have afghans here that  might begin to resist their rule so like  if some afghans want to leave the  taliban might not put that great  obstacles into their way  france what can we expect from the  taliban now will they be able to form a  government  the taliban minority factor in power  they have since they entered kabul um  conducted like talks with uh several  politicians that haven't fled the  country  reiterated that they want to have an  inclusive government but at the end of  the day their attitude is the attitude  of victors so they might include certain  figures in a government but uh at the  end they decide what will happen  um and and who will rule and how and  given that they have during the past 20  years always said  that the the constitution that was in  place the system that was in place  wasn't islamic or not islamic enough um  one can expect that there will be  radical changes and that they will  announce the government and try to  implement this and given that they  managed to topple the government they  certainly have the power to implement um  several things  journalist france marti reporting there  from kabul thank you very much for that  update  well let's get some analysis on  america's withdrawal now we can speak to  liz shulker she's a u.s political  analyst here in berlin  welcome to the show the u.s has  essentially now said that it is  withdrawn without getting all of its  people out  how big of a problem is that  the problem really depends on who you  are as secretary blinken said the number  of american civilians left in  afghanistan is in the low hundreds  however we have left thousands of our  afghan allies behind and although i love  to hear it's optimistic that they might  be allowed to leave the taliban have  lists and they have already as deutsche  bahnow's been going and trying to find  some of these folks and so i think it is  a big problem that america uh kind of  didn't live up to its promise to bring  its allies back with them well secretary  blinken has said this is the start of a  new diplomatic phase with afghanistan  what could that new phase look like  as we saw  ambassador ross was one of the absolute  last people to lead the country on the  absolute last flight out i worked at the  state department i know how passionate  um the civil servants there are about  the mission and about the country and so  they have tried to reiterate that this  mission is not over they're going to uh  have an embassy uh hopefully in in work  together uh with afghanistan and with  the taliban in qatar and i think uh  hopefully we'll see strength and  diplomatic ties kind of moving forward  there are a lot of people who have been  referring to this withdrawal as a  failure of the west but also of course  of the u.s as well how much you think  this has  damaged the us's credibility with its  allies  i don't think this is the first instance  this war has damaged credibility for  america i think this is rightfully a  wake-up call for america and the role  that it plays in the world both how  america sees itself as well as what the  world expects uh  the u.s can't come in with military  might and do nation building and instead  has to go back to those diplomatic  missions that we talked about and  hopefully the dedication of these civil  servants and the moving diplomatic um uh  actions will restore some of that  credibility and yet we saw this  extremely chaotic withdrawal in the last  phase and of course the horrific  suicide bombings at the airport that  killed us marines as well so what do you  think the fallout will be for president  biden  that's a really serious question i think  looking uh forward we have the midterms  coming up now congress people and  senators in their home states and their  home districts can kind of uh spin this  and present it in their own way there is  some distance between biden and uh  individuals in their districts but i  think biden needs to really move forward  um with strength and and talk about the  hundred and fourteen thousand people uh  that we have safely evacuated from the  country as well as really the progress  um diplomatically that we can see in the  next six months but it's absolutely  something that will hammer him moving  forward and he needs to remain  accountable for  u.s policy analyst liz shulker with us  thank you very much
 

